<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proofs</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime</h3>

<section id="A1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.1 </span>NTK: Neural Tangent Kernel</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, DNNs have a large number of parameters <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> compared to the number of data points <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, causing them to memorize data and adversely affecting their generalization ability.
Moreover, all data are subjected to nonlinear transformations, which results in the problem of minimizing a nonconvex objective function.
Due to these difficulties in analyzing the training dynamics of DNNs, the reasons for generalization of practical DNNs and the guarantee of global convergence remain open questions.
However, a theoretical framework called the neural tangent kernel (NTK) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacot et al., 2018</a>)</cite> has been developed to analyze the training dynamics of gradient descent in DNNs with sufficiently large width.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming that the loss function to be minimized in NN training is <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\frac{1}{2}\|f({x};\bm{\theta})-{y}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\frac{1}{2}\|f({x};\bm{\theta})-{y}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the parameters are updated by gradient descent with learning rate <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameter update <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\bm{\theta}=-\eta\frac{\partial{L}(\bm{\theta})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}=-\eta\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}{L}(\bm{\theta})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\bm{\theta}=-\eta\frac{\partial{L}(\bm{\theta})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}=-\eta\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}{L}(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Taking the continuous-time limit (where <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the training time), we obtain</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial\bm{\theta}}{\partial t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial\bm{\theta}}{\partial t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\eta\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}{L}(\bm{\theta}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\eta\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}{L}(\bm{\theta}(t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\eta J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})^{T}\nabla_{f_{t}({x})}{L}(f_{t}({x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\eta J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})^{T}\nabla_{f_{t}({x})}{L}(f_{t}({x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f({x};\bm{\theta})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}=\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}f_{t}({x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f({x};\bm{\theta})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}=\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}f_{t}({x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next, considering the time evolution of the function output rather than the parameters, we obtain</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!\frac{\partial f_{t}({x;\bm{\theta}})}{\partial t}\!\!\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!\frac{\partial f_{t}({x;\bm{\theta}})}{\partial t}\!\!\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\frac{\partial f_{t}({x;\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}\frac{\partial\bm{\theta}}{\partial t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\frac{\partial f_{t}({x;\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}\frac{\partial\bm{\theta}}{\partial t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!-\eta J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})^{T}\nabla_{f_{t}({x})}{L}(f_{t}({x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!-\eta J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})^{T}\nabla_{f_{t}({x})}{L}(f_{t}({x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!-\eta\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})\nabla_{f_{t}({x})}{L}(f_{t}({x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!-\eta\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})\nabla_{f_{t}({x})}{L}(f_{t}({x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})^{T}=\sum_{\text{layer}=1}^{\text{num of layer}}J_{t}\left({x};\bm{\theta}_{\text{layer}}\right)J_{t}\left({x};\bm{\theta}_{\text{layer}}\right)^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtext>layer</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>num of layer</mtext></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>layer</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>layer</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})J_{t}({x};\bm{\theta})^{T}=\sum_{\text{layer}=1}^{\text{num of layer}}J_{t}\left({x};\bm{\theta}_{\text{layer}}\right)J_{t}\left({x};\bm{\theta}_{\text{layer}}\right)^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The issue is that <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, the NTK at time <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, depends on <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, it has been shown that if the width of the randomly initialized NN is sufficiently large,
<math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})\approx\mathcal{K}_{0}({x},{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})\approx\mathcal{K}_{0}({x},{x})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacot et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Approximating the output of the neural network by its first-order Taylor expansion around the initial parameters, we have</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}^{\operatorname{lin}}\left(x;\bm{\theta}_{t}\right)\approx f_{0}\left(x;\bm{\theta}_{0}\right)+\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}f_{0}\left(x;\bm{\theta}_{0}\right)^{T}\left(\bm{\theta}_{t}-\bm{\theta}_{0}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>lin</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}^{\operatorname{lin}}\left(x;\bm{\theta}_{t}\right)\approx f_{0}\left(x;\bm{\theta}_{0}\right)+\nabla_{\bm{\theta}}f_{0}\left(x;\bm{\theta}_{0}\right)^{T}\left(\bm{\theta}_{t}-\bm{\theta}_{0}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Under this linearization, the training dynamics can be expressed in closed form:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{t}=\bm{\theta}_{0}-\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}({x})^{T}{\mathcal{K}}_{0}^{-1}\left(I-e^{-\eta{\mathcal{K}}_{0}t}\right)\left(f_{0}({x})-{y}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{t}=\bm{\theta}_{0}-\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}({x})^{T}{\mathcal{K}}_{0}^{-1}\left(I-e^{-\eta{\mathcal{K}}_{0}t}\right)\left(f_{0}({x})-{y}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{t}^{\operatorname{lin}}({x})=\left(I-e^{-\eta{\mathcal{K}}_{0}t}\right){y}+e^{-\eta{\mathcal{K}}_{0}t}f_{0}({x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>lin</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{t}^{\operatorname{lin}}({x})=\left(I-e^{-\eta{\mathcal{K}}_{0}t}\right){y}+e^{-\eta{\mathcal{K}}_{0}t}f_{0}({x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">NTK theory thus determines the dynamics of gradients in function space by introducing the NTK regime, which allows us to assume that the weights follow a Gaussian process even as training progresses. This is based on the result that a randomly initialized NN can be viewed as a Gaussian process when the hidden layer width becomes infinite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novak et al., 2018b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The NTK allows us to prove the global convergence of gradient descent; furthermore, the equivalence between the trained model and the Gaussian process can be used to explain the generalization performance of DNNs.
The NTK framework has been extended to CNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and RNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang, 2019</a>)</cite> in addition to MLPs, and exhaustive experiments have been conducted <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.2 </span>Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TIC requires that the statistical model be a regular model. However, DNNs are generally singular models.
The requirements for a regular model are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The posterior distribution of the parameters can be approximated by a Gaussian distribution, and the number of samples is sufficiently large (as n increases, the prior distribution is ignored).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-3.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is only one optimal solution <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmax\ell(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmax\ell(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-3.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In machine learning, we often seek to minimize the negative log-likelihood, treating it as a loss function.
Let <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be the predictive distribution of the model parameterized by <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\theta}}\in\bm{\Theta}\subset\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝚯</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\theta}}\in\bm{\Theta}\subset\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be the true distribution.
We can compare models by measuring the KL divergence between <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> to see how well <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> approximates <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{KL}(g,f)\!\!\!\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{KL}(g,f)\!\!\!\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\log\frac{g(y|x)}{f(y|x,\bm{\theta})}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\log\frac{g(y|x)}{f(y|x,\bm{\theta})}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log g(y|x)]-\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\bm{\theta})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log g(y|x)]-\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\bm{\theta})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since the first term of Equation <a href="#A1.E17" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> is independent of <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is better if it maximizes the second term,
i.e., the mean log-likelihood <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta})=\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\bm{\theta})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta})=\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\bm{\theta})]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The mean log-likelihood <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is an unknown quantity that cannot be computed directly, as it depends on the true data distribution <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, if a valid estimator of the mean log-likelihood can be obtained using the empirical distribution <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, it can serve as a criterion for evaluating the model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In model selection, we consider a DNN model with output distribution <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y|x,\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y|x,\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the parameters are estimated by maximum likelihood.
We obtain the fitted model <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y|x,\bm{\hat{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y|x,\bm{\hat{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing the unknown parameters <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with the maximum likelihood estimator <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\hat{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\hat{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\theta}}:=\argmax_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta}\ell(\bm{\theta})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\theta}}:=\argmax_{\bm{\theta}\in\Theta}\ell(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\bm{\theta})\!\!=\!\!\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\bm{\theta})]\!\!=\!\!\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\bm{\theta})\!\!=\!\!\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\bm{\theta})]\!\!=\!\!\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is the likelihood function over <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}\in\bm{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝚯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}\in\bm{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S_{n}}=\left\{(x_{1},y_{1}),(x_{2},y_{2}),\dots(x_{n},y_{n})\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>𝒏</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S_{n}}=\left\{(x_{1},y_{1}),(x_{2},y_{2}),\dots(x_{n},y_{n})\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the data observed according to the true data distribution <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> be the empirical distribution based on this <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>𝒏</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the law of large numbers,
<math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},\hat{\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},\hat{\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>
converges in probability to <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},\hat{\bm{\theta}})\xrightarrow[n\to+\infty]{}\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},\hat{\bm{\theta}})\xrightarrow[n\to+\infty]{}\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the estimator based on the empirical distribution in Equation <a href="#A1.E19" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> is a natural estimator of the mean log-likelihood.
While <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]</annotation></semantics></math> is a natural estimator of <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]</annotation></semantics></math>,
the parameter <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is itself estimated from the empirical data <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i})\sim\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i})\sim\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the same data are used both to estimate the parameters and to evaluate the mean log-likelihood of <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>, fair model selection is not possible without correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, it is necessary to evaluate and correct for this bias to enable fair model selection.
The bias of estimating the mean log-likelihood <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(y|x,\hat{\bm{\theta}})]</annotation></semantics></math> is formulated as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!b\!\!\!\!\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!b\!\!\!\!\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(\bm{y}|\bm{x},\hat{\bm{\theta}})]-\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(\bm{y}|\bm{x},\hat{\bm{\theta}})]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\mathbb{E}_{\hat{p}}[\log f(\bm{y}|\bm{x},\hat{\bm{\theta}})]-\mathbb{E}_{p}[\log f(\bm{y}|\bm{x},\hat{\bm{\theta}})]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\ell(\hat{\bm{\theta}})-\mathcal{L}(\hat{\bm{\theta}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\ell(\hat{\bm{\theta}})-\mathcal{L}(\hat{\bm{\theta}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\ell(\hat{\bm{\theta}})-\ell({\bm{\theta^{*}}})\right]\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\ell(\hat{\bm{\theta}})-\ell({\bm{\theta^{*}}})\right]\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\ell({\bm{\theta^{*}}})-\mathcal{L}({\bm{\theta^{*}}})\right]\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\ell({\bm{\theta^{*}}})-\mathcal{L}({\bm{\theta^{*}}})\right]\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\mathcal{L}({\bm{\theta^{*}}})-\mathcal{L}(\hat{\bm{\theta}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!n\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\mathcal{L}({\bm{\theta^{*}}})-\mathcal{L}(\hat{\bm{\theta}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximizer of <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, Equation <a href="#A1.E21" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> can be decomposed into Equations <a href="#A1.E22" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>, <a href="#A1.E23" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>, and <a href="#A1.E24" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>.
Moreover, Equation <a href="#A1.E23" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> converges to 0 because the expectation of <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\bm{\theta}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\bm{\theta}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> by the law of large numbers. Equations <a href="#A1.E22" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> and <a href="#A1.E24" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> each converge to <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{Tr}\left({\bm{H}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}})}^{-1}{\bm{D}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Tr</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{Tr}\left({\bm{H}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}})}^{-1}{\bm{D}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
This asymptotic validity holds under the regularity conditions of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">White (1982)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.3 </span>Applying the NTK Regime for TIC Derivation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can satisfy the above conditions by using the training dynamics of DNNs in the NTK regime.
Specifically, the NTK regime satisfies the first condition since it uses a locally linear approximation, as in Equation <a href="#A1.E13" title="In A.1.1 NTK: Neural Tangent Kernel ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, and treats the training of the NN as a Gaussian process.
In addition, as shown in Equation <a href="#A1.E14" title="In A.1.1 NTK: Neural Tangent Kernel ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, the optimal solution at the <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th step is uniquely determined, and the optimization is convex.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The positive definiteness of <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is proved in Appendix A4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacot et al. (2018)</a></cite>, under the assumption of non-polynomial Lipschitz nonlinearity.
From the definition of <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, the Fisher information matrix (FIM) is positive definite in the NTK regime because <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{t}({x},{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and the FIM share the same eigenvalues through a duality relationship.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This condition is true when the DNN is considered to be in the NTK regime, i.e., when Assumption <a href="#S2.Thmassum1" title="Assumption 2.1. ‣ 2.3 Deriving TIC as the Generalization Gap in the NTK Regime ‣ 2 Generalization Measures ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> is satisfied.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.1.3 Applying the NTK Regime for TIC Derivation ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a schematic diagram of the bias term <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.
The matrices <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{D}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{D}}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})</annotation></semantics></math> are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{rcl}{\bm{D}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\frac{\partial\log f(y|x,{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}\frac{\partial\log f(y|x,{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}={\bm{\theta^{*}}}}\right]\\
{\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\frac{\partial^{2}\log f(y|x,{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}={\bm{\theta^{*}}}}\right]\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{rcl}{\bm{D}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\frac{\partial\log f(y|x,{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}\frac{\partial\log f(y|x,{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}={\bm{\theta^{*}}}}\right]\\
{\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\mathbb{E}_{p}\left[\frac{\partial^{2}\log f(y|x,{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}={\bm{\theta^{*}}}}\right]\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If the true distribution <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is included in the assumed statistical model <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\bm{y}|\bm{x},\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\bm{y}|\bm{x},\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{D}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}={\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{D}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}={\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is valid and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\mathrm{Tr}(\bm{I})=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\mathrm{Tr}(\bm{I})=d</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the AIC can be derived.
In the DNN setting, this assumption does not hold, i.e., it is necessary to use the respective matrices for the misspecified situation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since bias <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the true data distribution <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, it needs to be estimated based on the observed data.
Assuming that the consistent estimators for <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{D}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{D}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}_{p}(\bm{{\theta^{*}}})}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}</annotation></semantics></math>,
respectively, the estimates in equation <a href="#A1.E20" title="In A.1.2 Preliminaries for TIC in NTK Regime ‣ A.1 Derivation of the TIC in NTK Regime ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{rcl}{\bm{C}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\partial\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}\frac{\partial\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}=\hat{\bm{\theta}}}\\
{\bm{H}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\partial^{2}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}=\hat{\bm{\theta}}}\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{rcl}{\bm{C}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\partial\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}}\frac{\partial\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}=\hat{\bm{\theta}}}\\
{\bm{H}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})}&amp;=&amp;\left.\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\partial^{2}\log f(y_{i}|x_{i},{\bm{\theta}})}{\partial\bm{\theta}\partial\bm{\theta}^{T}}\right|_{\bm{\theta}=\hat{\bm{\theta}}}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Using equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2 Information Matrix: Elements of Generalization Measures ‣ 2 Generalization Measures ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{b}</annotation></semantics></math> as an estimate of bias <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{b}=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\bm{H}(\bm{\hat{\theta})}^{-1}\bm{C}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{b}=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\bm{H}(\bm{\hat{\theta})}^{-1}\bm{C}(\bm{\hat{\theta}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is called Moody’s effective number of parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moody, 1992</a>)</cite>.
The TIC is derived in equation <a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by estimating the asymptotic bias of the mean log-likelihood with the log-likelihood of the statistical model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.23219v1/figs/theory/tic.png" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:275/242;" width="275" height="242" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>TIC takes into account the bias of the estimate.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Asymptotic Equivalence of TIC to Cross-Validation</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since deep learning usually requires a large amount of data and substantial training time, the holdout method is commonly used to divide the data into training data, validation data for model selection (especially hyperparameter optimization), and test data to verify model performance.
This method is relatively fast, but its evaluation varies depending on how the data are split, and it is not suitable when the dataset is small.
In <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-fold cross-validation, the entire training set is divided into <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> folds. One fold is used as validation data, while the remaining <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K-1</annotation></semantics></math> folds are used for training.
This process is repeated so that each fold serves as the validation set exactly once.
Leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) is the special case where each fold consists of a single data point.
LOOCV is empirically known to yield reliable estimates and is often used when the dataset is small.
If the number of data points is <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the bias of the estimation error is <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for the holdout method and <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for LOOCV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stone, 1977</a>)</cite>.
However, LOOCV requires <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> times the computational cost.
For a dataset such as ImageNet-1K with 1.2 million training images, the current practice is to use the holdout method, which achieves <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{n})</annotation></semantics></math> estimation error at low computational cost, rather than reducing the error to <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/{n})</annotation></semantics></math> at prohibitive computational expense.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>TIC Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Implementation and Environment for Experiment</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We performed our experiments with the ABCI supercomputer. For the ABCI supercomputer,
each node is composed of NVIDIA Tesla V100<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>4GPU and Intel Xeon Gold 6148 2.4 GHz, 20 Cores<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>2CPU. As a software environment, we used Red Hat 4.8.5, gcc 7.4, Python 3.6.5, PyTorch 1.6.0, cuDNN 7.6.2, and CUDA 10.0.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Hyperparameters and Detailed Configuration</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we report the hyperparameters’ search space.
We searched learning rate <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate decay rate <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and the timing to decay learning rate <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and regularization coefficient of weight decay <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.7</annotation></semantics></math>, the learning rate decays when training passes 70% of the total iterations.
Furthermore, a parameter to control momentum <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> was added to the hyperparameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To set the range in which to search for each hyperparameter, we followed the configurations of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al. (2019)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shallue et al. (2019)</a></cite>.
Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.2 Hyperparameters and Detailed Configuration ‣ Appendix C TIC Experimental Details ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> summarizes the workloads used in the experiment. We did not use batch normalization and the input image is simply normalized; no data augmentation was employed. The hyperparameter ranges are summarized in Tables <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.2 Hyperparameters and Detailed Configuration ‣ Appendix C TIC Experimental Details ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.2 Hyperparameters and Detailed Configuration ‣ Appendix C TIC Experimental Details ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.2 Hyperparameters and Detailed Configuration ‣ Appendix C TIC Experimental Details ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.2 Hyperparameters and Detailed Configuration ‣ Appendix C TIC Experimental Details ‣ Takeuchi’s Information Criteria as a Generalization Measure for Deep Neural Networks Close to the NTK Regime" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. We conducted a Bayesian optimization to explore hyperparameters in the range described in the tables.</p>
</div>
